Yes. The thing you’re actually trying to solve is an intertemporal allocation problem:

How much does present-you owe future-you, and how much does future-you owe present-you?

One correction first: the flea-in-a-jar story is widely used as a metaphor, but I couldn’t find solid evidence that the familiar viral version is a well-established scientific experiment. The better-supported analogue is learned helplessness / learned controllability: repeated uncontrollable stress can reduce later attempts to act, while experiences of genuine control can build resilience and restore active responding. 

So the useful model is:

present self
↔
future self

Present-you can spend comfort to buy future capability:

training
education
saving
practice
discipline
temporary discomfort

But future-you can also become a tyrant if every present moment is sacrificed to some imagined later payoff.

The inverse failure is:

present comfort maximized
→ future optionality depleted

So I’d make the governing principle Intertemporal Stewardship:

invest enough today to expand tomorrow
+
live enough today that tomorrow was worth building

And there’s a very good discriminator:

Is the discomfort producing a durable asset?

If suffering creates:

skill
health
capital
relationships
knowledge
resilience
optionality

then some present cost can be rational.

If it mostly produces:

damage
resentment
identity-binding
exhaustion
sunk cost
less future agency

then “future me will thank me” may just be a narrative covering bad allocation.

You can even give both selves veto rights:

Present Self veto: this is causing unacceptable damage now.

Future Self veto: this choice predictably steals too much capability from later me.

Everything between those boundaries is negotiable.

And your “little storm” idea has a clean version too. Sometimes people do need a disconfirming experience—not necessarily more suffering, but a situation where their action visibly changes the outcome:

old model: I can't
→ small controllable challenge
→ action works
→ prediction error
→ agency model updates

That is closer to learned controllability than simply shocking someone awake. Research increasingly emphasizes actual or perceived control over stressors as a key resilience mechanism. 

So maybe the golden rule is:

Train for future-you without making present-you merely the fuel.

You are the same continuity on both sides of the trade.